WEBVTT
TITLE Trees, Binary Search Trees and Heaps — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
A binary tree is a set of nodes where each node has at most two children, and one node is the root.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Depth counts edges down from the root. Height counts edges down to the deepest leaf. The root is at depth 0.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Count the nodes by how many children they have: n0 leaves, n1 with one child, n2 with two. Then n0 = n2 + 1, always.

4
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Full: every node has 0 or 2 children. Complete: every level is filled except possibly the last, which fills left to right. Perfect: both at once.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
A traversal is a fixed order of visiting the nodes. The four you need differ only in when the root is visited.

6
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Preorder is root first, so the first key you print is the root of the whole tree.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Inorder gives 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 here, and postorder gives 1, 3, 2, 5, 7, 6, 4.

8
00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Preorder plus inorder rebuilds the tree exactly. Preorder plus postorder does not: the two-node tree is already ambiguous.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.000
A binary search tree promises this: every key in the left subtree is smaller than the root, and every key in the right subtree is larger.

10
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:15.000
One consequence you will use constantly: the inorder traversal of a search tree is sorted.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Here is a tree that passes the parent-child test everywhere and is still not a search tree: 12 sits in the left subtree of 10.

12
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:30.000
A search path is a sequence of left and right decisions. Read it as bounds: each step narrows the range the target may lie in.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Insertion is a search that fails. Walk down comparing, and hang the new key where the walk falls off the tree.

14
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Same six keys, two orders, two trees. The set of keys does not determine the shape; the insertion order does.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:53.000
Sorted insertion gives a chain, so search becomes a walk: n comparisons, height n-1. That is the worst case.

16
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:00.000
With 15 nodes the smallest possible height is 3 and the largest is 14. Both are reachable.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.000
To count the trees on n nodes, split at the root: if the left subtree has k-1 nodes, the right has n-k.

18
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:15.000
The Catalan numbers are 1, 1, 2, 5, 14, 42, 132, 429. The formula is 2n choose n, divided by n+1.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Three distinct keys give 5 search trees; four keys give 14. The number of search trees equals the number of shapes.

20
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:30.000
If the shape is given and labelled, there is only one filling. If the keys are labelled and the shape is free, Catalan counts.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Deleting a leaf is free. Deleting a node with one child splices that child into its place.

22
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:45.000
A node with two children cannot be spliced. Copy in its inorder successor, the leftmost node of its right subtree, and delete that node instead.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:53.000
Successor and predecessor are also how you answer "the k-th smallest" and "the next key after x".

24
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Every deletion case costs the height: one walk down, and at most one more walk down the successor subtree.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.000
A heap makes two promises: the shape is complete, and every parent is no larger than its children.

26
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:15.000
In an array indexed from 0, the children of i are 2i+1 and 2i+2, and the parent is (i-1)/2 rounded down.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:23.000
In a min-heap the largest key must be a leaf, because any internal node has a child at least as large. So there are as many candidate positions as leaves.

28
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:30.000
How deep can the 9th smallest key go? At most 8 edges, because every ancestor must be smaller and there are only 8 smaller keys.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.000
Insert: put the new key at the next free position, which keeps the shape, then swap it upward while it is smaller than its parent.

30
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Remove the minimum: move the last leaf to the root, shrink the array, then swap downward against the smaller child.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.000
Both operations cost the height, which for a complete tree of n nodes is at most log2(n+1) - 1, so O(log n).

32
00:03:52.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Deleting an arbitrary element is not a heap operation, but it is a fixed recipe: replace it with the last leaf, then sift up or down, whichever the comparison demands.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.000
Building a heap by n insertions costs n log n. Building it bottom-up costs n, and the difference is not a constant factor.

34
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Sum the work by height: about n/4 nodes move one level, n/8 move two, and the total is at most 2n.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.000
Start at the last internal node and sift down each one, working back to the root. The array becomes a heap in one pass.

36
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:30.000
Heapsort: build in n, then remove the minimum n times at log n each. n log n in place, and not stable.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.000
A sorted array searches in log n and inserts in n. A search tree trades those. A heap gives up search entirely.

38
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:44.000
Find the minimum in constant time: a heap. Find an arbitrary key: a search tree or a hash table. Report keys in order: a search tree.

39
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:52.000
Four questions decide almost every tree question: which promise is stated, is the shape fixed, is the order fixed, and which base index is in force.

40
00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Next: balanced trees, then graphs. The pointer surgery from the linked-list lesson is the same surgery a rotation uses.

